                        "form": "узурпа́торе"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "28d1bb92-dbd3-4d6b-98fc-3ad2eff1dbc0",
                        "entry_id": "2c688e9c-b2f0-3a1d-af76-cf0133c72498",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural",
                            "prepositional"
                        ],
                        "form": "узурпа́торах"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "9a0d17aa-67f5-3d64-a2f1-6598d24fecb3",
                        "entry_id": "2c688e9c-b2f0-3a1d-af76-cf0133c72498",
                        "glosses": [
                            "книжн. человек, незаконно захвативший в свои руки власть или присвоивший (узурпировавший) чужие права на что-либо"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "21d33fc2-d543-4984-9dc9-a54817d8d48f",
                                "sense_id": "9a0d17aa-67f5-3d64-a2f1-6598d24fecb3",
                                "text": "Как быть: простота, искренность ― удел законных государей; кокетство, лицедейство и лицемерие ― удел узурпаторов, т. е. государей поддельных."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a5fb4db3-d6ae-44fe-abb2-9e64e1f61fb6",
                                "sense_id": "9a0d17aa-67f5-3d64-a2f1-6598d24fecb3",
                                "text": "Ломов. По-вашему выходит, значит, что я узурпатор? Сударыня, никогда я чужих земель не захватывал и обвинять меня в этом никому не позволю…"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "64686e53-4abb-41d7-9396-6f0a2590b2f5",
                                "sense_id": "9a0d17aa-67f5-3d64-a2f1-6598d24fecb3",
                                "text": "― Эй, подвинься! ― решительно сказала я и попыталась сдвинуть узурпатора хотя бы на другую половину сиденья."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "a657c1ce-7368-4efc-8d0e-62e4dd816b36",
                        "source_entry_id": "2c688e9c-b2f0-3a1d-af76-cf0133c72498",
                        "source_sense_id": "9a0d17aa-67f5-3d64-a2f1-6598d24fecb3",
                        "target_lang_word": "usurper",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "usurper",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "d9066285-b6b2-3d7c-935e-99bb233245ae",
                                        "entry_id": "8bdaf719-0ba1-3c22-8225-b43084e12467",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "One who usurps."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4114cee5-629c-4dda-afbd-8cecee6f14bb",
                                                "sense_id": "d9066285-b6b2-3d7c-935e-99bb233245ae",
                                                "text": "The fact that the Yongle emperor was therefore a usurper, regicide and nepoticide (nephew-killer) made compiling the Veritable Record – or official history – of his reign a most dangerous scholarly post. The official given this ticklish task managed to survive several drafts, finally producing one that pleased his master as it omitted the dead nephew's reign altogether."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "1605991a-08fc-4ce7-8e49-7e6a6c7cbc2c",
                        "source_entry_id": "2c688e9c-b2f0-3a1d-af76-cf0133c72498",
                        "source_sense_id": "9a0d17aa-67f5-3d64-a2f1-6598d24fecb3",
                        "target_lang_word": "usurpress",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "usurpress",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9b11948b-7942-3f20-91d6-fa438ce84641",
                                        "entry_id": "ee801680-f69b-30c1-9748-0fd7285f98b1",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A female usurper."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "rare"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "34584303-119b-4a8c-876c-4dfadf192c4f",
                                                "sense_id": "9b11948b-7942-3f20-91d6-fa438ce84641",
                                                "text": "This yet may dash the Marriage; and Leucasia That bold Usurpress of my Bed shall miss Of being saluted Queen to night howe'r."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "704ce740-416d-4f1a-8283-369b4c00e52a",
                                                "sense_id": "9b11948b-7942-3f20-91d6-fa438ce84641",
                                                "text": "Madame Le Crampon was the absolute ruler of this machine;--her mother had ceased to hold almost any relation to it! She had abdicated in favour of that hard and implacable usurpress."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "205a6804-1650-41d4-b37a-8dcac1d321d9",
                                                "sense_id": "9b11948b-7942-3f20-91d6-fa438ce84641",
                                                "text": "1868, Anonymous, Pandora (poem), The Atlantic Monthly, Vol 22, Issue 132, archived here\nThou, that assumest to lead,\nHolding the truth and the keys of the skies,\nArt the usurpress indeed,\nAnd rulest thy sons with a sceptre of lies."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "53562e8b-b08a-4a7f-b9f1-247b7b7cfd91",
                                                "sense_id": "9b11948b-7942-3f20-91d6-fa438ce84641",
                                                "text": "Sophia, who was anxious not to be reckoned a usurpress and who wished to keep up appearances, held two thrones and two crowns on behalf of Ivan and Peter."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0fd9b321-109c-43d0-9dd1-98e3c9cc4ff8",
                                                "sense_id": "9b11948b-7942-3f20-91d6-fa438ce84641",
                                                "text": "I would \"attend\" Auschwitz with invisible resources that vastly increased the chances of survival, resources that included even my linguistic abilities, since German was my mother tongue, so to speak, and French my vernacular, while English was the language I had spoken with my brother and studied successfully in school. Finally, Russian was the rule with my father, sister, and the usurpress, and I was literally at home in it."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "00fecf0d-ab2f-4a5c-8b47-8466a4c43894",
                                                "sense_id": "9b11948b-7942-3f20-91d6-fa438ce84641",
                                                "text": "I have a number of these. They were issued in March 1893 and advertised the journal \"The Jacobite\", organ of the Legitimist Jacobite League. They are listed in \"Scottish Stamp and Label Catalogue 1970\" and subsequently illustrated in \"Scottish Stamp News\" and \"Cinderella Philatelist\" There are used covers in existence which show the usurpress stamp upside down with the 'stamp' of Queen Mary IV and III alongside."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
